            <ArticleTitle>[Lipid peroxidation and the response of the antioxidant defense system in the obese type 2 diabetic compared with the non-obese type 2 diabetic].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1905-11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3305/nutr hosp.v28in06.6946</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Diabetes is associated with increased lipid peroxidation, quantified as the levels of thiobarbituric acid reactive substances (TBARS). In parallel, the antioxidant defense system (ADS) reacts to diminish the oxidative damage.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the levels of lipid peroxidation and the activity of antioxidant enzymes in obese type 2 diabetic (DM2) individuals compared to non-obese DM2 individuals.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Lipid peroxidation was quantified by measuring TBARS and the ADS response by measuring the activity of superoxide dismutase (SOD), catalase (CAT) and glutathione peroxidase (GPx).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Two groups of 30 subjects were studied. The obese DM2 group had a mean body mass index (BMI) 38.6 ± 3.5 kg m(-2) compared to the control group 24.7 ± 3.6 kg m(-2) (p&lt;0.01). TBARS levels in the study group were higher compared to the control group (p &lt;0.01). Multiple linear regression analysis suggested that activities of SOD and CAT adjusted to lipid peroxidation (TBARS) in the obese DM2 individuals.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">TBARS levels suggest greater oxidative damage in obese DM2 subjects with a diminished response of ADS.</AbstractText>
